WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.868
Commas and FANBOYS

00:00:02.248 --> 00:00:07.257
Comma + FANBOYS joins two sentences. A comma alone never does.

00:00:08.457 --> 00:00:09.455
How to spot it

00:00:09.855 --> 00:00:12.230
Commas moving around and, but, so

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.609
Or around however, therefore

00:00:15.159 --> 00:00:18.596
It’s testing: how to join two complete sentences

00:00:19.796 --> 00:00:20.591
The method

00:00:20.991 --> 00:00:23.504
Step 1: Split at the joining word

00:00:23.784 --> 00:00:24.976
Look at both sides.

00:00:25.526 --> 00:00:28.013
Step 2: New subject after it?

00:00:28.293 --> 00:00:31.921
Both sides are sentences: comma before FANBOYS.

00:00:32.471 --> 00:00:35.420
Step 3: Same subject, two verbs?

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:36.509
No comma.

00:00:37.059 --> 00:00:39.355
Step 4: However or therefore?

00:00:39.635 --> 00:00:43.248
Not FANBOYS. Semicolon or period before it.

00:00:43.798 --> 00:00:44.908
In a few words

00:00:45.058 --> 00:00:47.765
New subject? Comma + FANBOYS.

00:00:48.965 --> 00:00:49.787
The rules

00:00:50.187 --> 00:00:50.973
FANBOYS

00:00:51.253 --> 00:00:54.442
for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so

00:00:54.722 --> 00:00:57.280
Right: I ran, but I missed the bus.

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:00.824
One subject, two verbs: no comma

00:01:01.104 --> 00:01:03.623
Wrong: I ran, and missed the bus.

00:01:03.903 --> 00:01:05.971
Right: I ran and missed the bus.

00:01:06.521 --> 00:01:08.120
However is not FANBOYS

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.282
Wrong: It rained, however, we played.

00:01:11.562 --> 00:01:14.577
Right: It rained; however, we played.

00:01:15.127 --> 00:01:16.914
Comma after an intro clause

00:01:17.194 --> 00:01:19.621
Right: When the bell rang, we left.

00:01:20.821 --> 00:01:21.862
Let’s try one

00:01:22.262 --> 00:01:27.375
The first prototype of the solar car failed to finish the race ______ the second,

00:01:27.375 --> 00:01:30.204
lighter model completed the course in record time.

00:01:30.854 --> 00:01:36.004
Left side: a full sentence. Right side: another full sentence.

00:01:36.577 --> 00:01:41.737
However isn’t a FANBOYS word, so a comma alone can’t join them.

00:01:41.737 --> 00:01:44.278
And no punctuation at all is a run-on.

00:01:44.828 --> 00:01:48.761
Semicolon before however, comma after. Choice B.

00:01:49.961 --> 00:01:50.956
Common traps

00:01:51.356 --> 00:01:52.816
The compound verb comma

00:01:53.096 --> 00:01:55.434
No new subject? No comma.

00:01:55.984 --> 00:01:57.182
However with commas

00:01:57.462 --> 00:01:58.709
Still a comma splice.

00:01:59.909 --> 00:02:01.176
Commas and FANBOYS

00:02:01.456 --> 00:02:04.163
New subject? Comma + FANBOYS.

00:02:04.713 --> 00:02:05.486
Spot it

00:02:05.686 --> 00:02:09.585
Commas around and / but / so, or however / therefore

00:02:10.135 --> 00:02:10.842
Steps

00:02:11.042 --> 00:02:13.008
1. Split at the joining word

00:02:13.288 --> 00:02:17.478
2. New subject after it? → comma + FANBOYS

00:02:17.758 --> 00:02:21.646
3. Same subject, two verbs? → no comma

00:02:21.926 --> 00:02:26.220
4. however / therefore → ; before, comma after

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:27.523
Remember

00:02:27.723 --> 00:02:32.044
FANBOYS: for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so

00:02:32.324 --> 00:02:35.191
I ran and missed the bus. (no comma)

00:02:35.471 --> 00:02:37.793
It rained; however, we played.

00:02:38.073 --> 00:02:39.860
Comma after an intro clause

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:41.015
Trap

00:02:41.215 --> 00:02:43.799
Commas alone around “however” = splice

00:02:44.549 --> 00:02:48.244
